it was 61 years ago that patrice lumumba  was executed by a firing squad in  democratic republic of congo with help  from belgian mercenaries  the belgian police officer cut his body  into pieces and dissolved it in a barrel  of acid but kept his gold tooth as a  trophy and took it back to belgium  at a ceremony in brussels on monday the  government returned the tooth to  delegates from congo and members of  lumumba's family  i would like here in the presence of his  family to reiterate the belgian  government's apologies for the way it  weighed at the time on the decision to  put an end to the days of the first  prime minister of the congo  belgium and its royal family earned vast  wealth from colonizing congo and  violently exploiting its people and its  natural resources  millions of people were killed  patrice lumumba became wildly popular as  he campaigned for independence from  belgium colonial rule  but belgium and the us didn't like his  democratic and socialist ideas and  supported those who killed him shortly  after independence  the mumbai's relatives have been  requesting the return of the tooth for  years  it's the only known remaining piece of  his body which they want to later rest  i can't say it's joyous but it is  positive for us it's positive that we  can finally bury one of ours  two weeks ago belgian king philippe  visited congo and expressed regret for  belgium's colonial past  he stopped short of apologizing  belgium's been trying to improve its  public image regarding its brutal  colonial past since the black lives  matter movement began two years ago  the movement prompted activists to  deface statues of king leopold ii in  belgium who colonised congo in the 1880s  some have since been removed belgium is  willing to take very symbolic gestures  very simple and easy acts but the acts  of repairing the acts of  allowing justice to to happen this is  not happening  the returning of lumumba's tooth is  certainly symbolic  congo still suffers from the kind of  brutal violence and exploitation of  natural resources that belgian  colonialists began there more than a  century ago  a ceremony in brussels won't change that  malcolm webb al jazeera
 

